.. and that's what I was disagreeing with - I guess you just misread my disagreement :)


>  There are special permissions in the \Program Files tree which can cause problems if you
> use it to store your packages. R will set up a personal library for
> you automatically if you let it so there is no reason not to use it

... there is: it's non-standard (only the system library is guaranteed to exist) and that's where the problems come from.


> and you will avoid the permissions problems with \Program Files.  Also
> that is presumably the preferred configuration or else why would it
> set up such a library by default.
> 
> Regarding specifying paths, I think there are some bugs in jgr.exe
> that require that you specify the library paths in certain cases even
> when you might think not.  On my old machine unless I specify --home,
> --libpaths, R_LIBS and R_LIBS_USER jgr won't run properly but instead
> loads a library from an ancient version of R that I also have on the
> same machine. The only way I could get it to reliably run was to
> specify everything.
> 

JGR launcher assumes that you will be using the system location. If you are not, you'll have to do some legwork and tell it what you really want. The main problem is that the launcher can't figure out what R will be using without running R but yet it has to setup the environment before running R. It's the unfortunate mess of Windows where R is setup to work like all unix systems (essentially assuming it's on the PATH), but that doesn't work on Windows. The only way to try to guess which R to run is to ask the registry, but that doesn't have any information on the additional settings such as library paths. So in the end you have multiple information sources fighting each other ... a real mess ... At any rate, if someone wants to clean up that mess in some reasonable way, patches are welcome
 .

Cheers,
